
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:fe_web@

%9Parameters:%9

    STATUS:     Displays whether the server is enabled, the number of
                connected clients and, for each client, its address, its
                server tag and the number of messages sent and received.

    Without any parameter, the status is displayed.

%9Description:%9

    Shows the status of fe-web, the WebSocket server that lets a web client
    show and use this erssi session from a browser.
    The fe_web module is loaded at startup by default (it is listed in the
    autoload_modules setting) when erssi is built with fe-web support.

    The server itself is controlled by the settings below. Changing any of
    them starts, stops or restarts the server, which disconnects the web
    clients; changing other settings does not touch it.

        fe_web_enabled (default: OFF)
            Runs the server.
        fe_web_port (default: 9001)
            The TCP port to listen on.
        fe_web_bind (default: 127.0.0.1)
            The address to listen on.
        fe_web_socket (default: empty)
            A Unix socket to listen on instead of fe_web_bind and
            fe_web_port; empty listens on TCP. "~/" stands for the home
            directory and a relative path is relative to the erssi
            directory (~/.erssi), so "fe-web.sock" is
            ~/.erssi/fe-web.sock.
        fe_web_password (default: empty)
            The password a client must give. The server refuses to start
            while it is empty.

    Connections always use TLS (wss://) with a self-signed certificate,
    and the messages are additionally encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a
    key derived from fe_web_password. The certificate and its key are made
    the first time the server starts and kept in ~/.erssi/fe-web-cert.pem
    and ~/.erssi/fe-web-key.pem (mode 0600); a key that other users can
    read, or that is not yours, is replaced with a new one. Nothing is
    written while fe_web_enabled is OFF.

    Keep fe_web_bind on 127.0.0.1 and put the web client behind a TLS proxy
    or tunnel if it has to be reached from elsewhere.

    On a shared shell box every local user can connect to a port on
    127.0.0.1. A Unix socket can be reached by you alone: the socket file
    is made with mode 0600 and, on Linux and the BSDs, connections from
    processes of other users are refused as well. TLS and the password
    work on it exactly as on TCP; the web client connects to the socket
    path and checks the certificate for the name localhost.

    The directory of the socket must be yours and writable by nobody else
    (a missing one is made with mode 0700), otherwise the server does not
    start. A socket left behind by an erssi that did not exit cleanly is
    replaced; any other file at the path is left alone and the server does
    not start. The socket is removed when the server stops.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET fe_web_password a-long-random-secret
    /SET fe_web_bind 127.0.0.1
    /SET fe_web_enabled ON
    /SET fe_web_socket ~/.erssi/fe-web.sock
    /SET -clear fe_web_socket
    /FE_WEB
    /FE_WEB STATUS

%9See also:%9 LOAD, SET

